So, I was reading the recent discussion about YANI on how to differentiate
the classes.

I came up with this general thought on *why* the classes end up similar to
each other as it stands now and how to make them stand out agaisnt each
other.  Note that we are talking about mostly end game.

First, my contention is that main source/reason why they end up looking like
the same thing is that nethack's over-reliance on items and equipments.  I'm
somewhat torn on this being 'the problem' since I find the major reason why
I enjoy nethack is how different objects in the game interacts with each
other and environment, and the challenge of identifying them.  However, most
of you realize now that most long lasting/ascendable characters have very
similar set of equipments.

If you think about it, given proper equipment and "the Bell", lvl 1
character would be pretty much as effective as lvl 30 character and be able
to ascend.  It's highlightened by the fact that pacifism is a doable
conduct, and pretty much the only reason why you want to level up as a
pacifist is to be able to do the quest and get some hps/pps.  Both hps and
pps can also be obtained through alchemy and other sources as well.  So
items really end up dominating 'the end game' since you generally have
enough resources to make even a lvl 1 character effective.

To further the discussion, in general there are 3 'arch types' of characters
at the end.  Combat Wombat, Spell Caster, and hybrid.  What differentiate
them, at this point, obviously, is the access to the magic spells and
firepower you can get from weapons.  This is also where you end up wanting
to go much higher than lvl 14, since spells gain more power as your level
goes up, more skill points you get to distribute into various magic skills,
and spells become easier to cast.

On the other side of spectrum, what makes combat wombats work is the access
to the powerful weapons/weapon skills.  To this end, there's a huge
difference between characters who can get a hands on some top notch artifact
weapons (especially Grayswandir) and whether or not you can #twoweapon.
Again, one of the big item here is the presence of #twoweapon skill that
differentiate the classes that have it, and classes that doesn't.

So, looking at this, I contend one of the obvious way you can make class
differentiation work is to have more "skills" that's useful *At the end
game*.  The other spectrum of this is to restrict the access of various
powerful items to certain classes but I would like to avoid that since I
think it's one of the most interesting thing about nethack is the
interaction with items/objects.

Let's look at some existing skills.  Aside from magic skills which is fairly
well developped, first obvious targets I think are
Riding/bare-handed/martial skills.  All of those are woefully unutilized.
Making riding interesting alone make Knights stand out from other classes,
and we all know martial skill is a wee bit underwhelming compared to the
two-weaponing wombats or double-damaging wombats.  I don't even have any
idea if anyone actually intentionally work on bare-handed skill.  I think
giving some interesting side effects to those skills when you hit (rather
than giving them "I hit as hard as using weapons) would be interesting
alternative.  Sort of like Magicbane, but let the martial artists choose
what effect they want.  Disarming, choking, blinding, stunning, confusing,
pushing back, draining life, range attacking, etcs would give them
interesting tactical choises.

Another skill that might be interesting to work on is the Whip skill.  This
obviously give Archeologist something.  It doesn't have to be a combat
related skill either.  Maybe give them limited jump when you get to certain
skill level.  But this also must be good enough to give a reason to work on
the skill so you get to use it in the end game.

There are a few other new skills that we could devise.  Alchemy could make
into a skill.  We had that 'gem potion' idea.  Maybe introduce a now skill
related to it.  Stealth can definitely be made into a skill, as well as
tracking/disarming traps.  I think we had foraging idea a while ago.  Maybe
animal handling skill?  Anatomy skill?  Singing/Performing?  Prayer skill
perhaps?  You get the picture.

Obviously, we'll have to find a way to balance these with existing structure
of nethack and stuff.  But we can always play test it on Slash'em or someone
come up with an unofficial patch so we can work with them and properly
balance'em (remember, prefarably all those skills should be useful in end
game to make an impact while not making the game too easy...).  But I
personally think those are a good way to differentiate each classes, plus
introducing some incentive to gain more exp levels.

So this is the brainstorm I had.  I think this is a discussion worth
exploring...Plus I like this sort of game related discussion :D

